This paper investigates the normalized ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific constraints and growth conditions for $\beta$, $\lambda$, and $f$, and we leverage a homotopy-based minimax principle to establish the existence of a normalized ground state solution for this equation. This research also explores the possibility of solution existence for a related equation lacking the symmetry property found in the biharmonic Choquard equation. To facilitate this investigation, an informal analysis of the equation's variational structure is provided. Moreover, we demonstrate the normal ground state solution can be obtained through a direct application of the minimax principle, and discuss the implications of this approach. To further illustrate the technique's effectiveness, we construct an example satisfying the given conditions, and present a corresponding normalized ground state solution for the associated equation. This research was initially presented as a part of the candidate's doctoral dissertation.

